To achieve true ultra-low-latency (ULL) performance, the modern standard is to avoid passing packets up to user-space unless absolutely necessary. 

The implementation below uses **eBPF/XDP (eXpress Data Path)** written in **C**. This approach processes packets directly inside the Linux kernel network driver (at the network interface card's ring buffer), bypassing the entire Linux network stack. 

This architecture guarantees **sub-microsecond (nanosecond-scale) processing latencies** and allows for line-rate filtering and forwarding (e.g., 10Gbps/40Gbps+) on commodity hardware.

