If you send a UDP packet on port `9999` to this machine, it will immediately rewrite the MAC/IP headers and fire it back to the sender within **nanoseconds**, entirely bypassing your Linux kernel's routing, socket layers, and user-space contexts.

To achieve **ultra-low-latency** packet filtering and forwarding, we must bypass the standard Linux kernel network stack. The industry standard for doing this directly in the OS kernel without the overhead of copying packets to user space is **eBPF/XDP (eXpress Data Path)**. 

XDP executes our C code directly inside the network driver, processing packets straight out of the Network Interface Card (NIC) ring buffer before any memory allocation (like `sk_buff`) or context switching occurs.

Below is a complete, production-grade initial implementation of an ultra-low-latency packet filter and forwarder written in **C**.

