### 3. Key Performance-Critical Optimizations Explained

1.  **`snaplen(64)` (Snap Length):**
    We configure the hardware NIC to only copy the first 64 bytes of each packet to RAM. Because we only care about the IP and TCP/UDP headers (5-tuple), ignoring the payload dramatically reduces PCI Express and memory bus saturation.
2.  **`#[inline(always)]` on `parse_packet`:**
    Instructs the compiler to merge the packet parsing code directly into the main loop, eliminating the overhead of function call stack frames on every packet.
3.  **`slice::try_into()` Conversion:**
    Modern Rust compilers optimize safe slice conversions (e.g., `packet[12..16].try_into()`) directly into vectorized CPU register load instructions (`mov` or `vmov`), bypassing any bounds-checking overhead.
4.  **`FxHashMap`:**
    Rust’s default standard library map uses SipHash to defend against hash collision attacks. In performance-critical network engineering, this is unnecessary overhead. Using `FxHash` (a fast additive hash function) cuts lookup and insert times in half.
5.  **Batched OS Buffering:**
    We configure a huge `16MB` kernel ring buffer (`buffer_size`) and disable immediate delivery (`immediate_mode(false)`). This allows the OS driver to pass chunks of packets to our user-space application at once, reducing system call context switches.

