3. **Hysteresis Prevention:** To prevent an actuator (like a motor, heater, or valve) from burning out by rapidly switching on and off due to sensor noise right at the threshold, a 5% hysteresis band is calculated.
4. **EMA Filtering:** An EMA filter requires keeping only **one** previous value in memory. A standard Moving Average filter requires storing an array/buffer of the last $N$ samples, which wastes precious RAM on an edge microcontroller.

To demonstrate code designed for a truly resource-constrained edge system (such as an 8-bit or 32-bit microcontroller without a Floating Point Unit), we will use **C**. 

This implementation avoids expensive floating-point math entirely, instead utilizing **fixed-point integer math** and **bitwise operations** to implement an Exponential Moving Average (EMA) filter. It also includes **hysteresis** to prevent the actuator from rapidly toggling (chattering) when the sensor data hovers near the threshold.

