        printf("Tick %02d | Raw Temp: %5.2f C | Filtered Temp: %5.2f C | Actuator: %s\n", 
               tick, raw_temp, filtered_temp, actuator_state ? "ON" : "OFF");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### Why this code suits highly constrained systems:
1. **Low-Pass Filter Memory Efficiency**: Standard moving averages require storing an array of the last $N$ readings (e.g., storing 50 floats takes 200 bytes of RAM). The **EMA filter** used here uses exactly **4 bytes** of memory total, regardless of how "strong" the filter is.
2. **Hysteresis Actuator Control**: Rather than triggering on a single temperature (like $40^\circ\text{C}$), the code uses a $42^\circ\text{C}$ "turn-on" threshold and a $38^\circ\text{C}$ "turn-off" threshold. This prevents a relay/motor from turning on and off dozens of times a second due to tiny sub-degree fluctuations.
3. **No Garbage Collection (No Jitter)**: The timing is perfectly deterministic. Every single control loop iteration executes in the exact same number of clock cycles, crucial for real-time safety.

