### Part 1: The C++ Native Plugin (`PhysicsEngine.cpp`)

This code defines the physics structures and the calculation step. It uses standard C linkage (`extern "C"`) so that Unity/C# can easily communicate with it.

```cpp
// PhysicsEngine.cpp
#define EXPORT_API __declspec(dllexport)

// Match Unity's Vector3 memory layout
struct Vector3 {
    float x;
    float y;
    float z;
};

// Represents a simple spherical rigid body
struct RigidBody {
    Vector3 position;
    Vector3 velocity;
    float mass;
    float radius;
    float bounciness; // Coefficient of restitution (0.0 = no bounce, 1.0 = perfect bounce)
};

extern "C" {
    // Updates the physics simulation for a single timestep (dt)
    EXPORT_API void StepSimulation(RigidBody* body, float dt, float gravityY) {
        if (body == nullptr || dt <= 0.0f) return;

        // 1. Apply Gravity (Acceleration)
        body->velocity.y += gravityY * dt;

        // 2. Integrate Position (Euler Integration)
        body->position.x += body->velocity.x * dt;
        body->position.y += body->velocity.y * dt;
        body->position.z += body->velocity.z * dt;

        // 3. Collision Detection & Resolution (Static Floor at Y = 0)
        float floorLevel = 0.0f;
        float bottomOfSphere = body->position.y - body->radius;

        if (bottomOfSphere < floorLevel) {
            // Penetration depth
            float penetration = floorLevel - bottomOfSphere;

            // Resolve Penetration (Push sphere out of the floor)
            body->position.y += penetration;

            // Resolve Velocity (Bounce)
            // Reverse Y velocity and apply coefficient of restitution
            if (body->velocity.y < 0.0f) {
                body->velocity.y = -body->velocity.y * body->bounciness;

                // Friction: dampen horizontal movement upon contact
                body->velocity.x *= 0.95f; 
                body->velocity.z *= 0.95f;

                // Rest threshold to stop micro-bouncing jitter
                if (body->velocity.y < 0.1f) {
                    body->velocity.y = 0.0f;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

*To use this, compile it as a shared library (`.dll` for Windows, `.dylib` for macOS, or `.so` for Linux) named `CustomPhysicsEngine`.*

