3. Attach the `CustomPhysicsBody` script to the Sphere.
4. Position the Sphere at $Y = 5$ (so it has room to fall).
5. Hit **Play**. The Sphere will accelerate downwards due to gravity, hit the ground ($Y=0$), and bounce back up according to the set `Bounciness` value.

To create a commercial-grade physics plugin that works across engines (Unity, Godot, or Unreal), the industry-standard architecture is to write a **Native C++ Dynamic Link Library (DLL/.so/.dylib)** with a **C-Compatible Application Binary Interface (ABI)**. 

This approach allows your core physics simulation to run at maximum native C++ speeds, while engine-side scripts (like C# in Unity/Godot) can easily call into it using simple wrapper bindings with zero garbage collection overhead.

Below is the complete implementation of a custom 3D particle physics plugin:

