To create a practical, high-performance physics plugin that can be used across commercial engines, the industry-standard approach is to write the core physics engine in **C++** and compile it as a dynamic library (`.dll` on Windows, `.dylib` on macOS, `.so` on Linux). 

This library exposes a flat **C API**, which can then be easily imported into **Unity (via C# P/Invoke)**, **Unreal Engine (via C++ Bindings)**, or **Godot (via GDExtension)**.

