### Architectural Details

1. **Fixed Timestep Accumulator:** The loop decouples actual processing time from simulated game time. If the framework experiences a brief lag spike (e.g., system I/O), the `while (accumulator >= dt)` catches up by stepping the physics sub-frame multiple times before the next render draw occurs. This prevents objects from clipping through walls at low frame-rates.
2. **Semi-Implicit Euler Integration:**
   $$v_{t+1} = v_t + a \cdot dt$$
   $$x_{t+1} = x_t + v_{t+1} \cdot dt$$
   Using the new velocity $v_{t+1}$ rather than the previous one $v_t$ inside the position update provides energy conservation stability superior to explicit forward Euler integration at negligible computational costs.
3. **Impulse-Based Elastic Collisions:** When spheres collide, physical parameters (momentum, restitution) are solved instantaneously along the collision normal, modifying both translational velocities perfectly while managing penetrations to prevent visual overlap glitching.

This is a complete, self-contained C++ implementation of a real-time 2D rigid body physics engine. 

To address the **frame-rate constraints** and prevent physics glitching (like objects passing through walls at lower frame rates), the code uses a **fixed-timestep accumulator loop** (the "Fix Your Timestep" algorithm). This separates the physics simulation tick rate (which runs at a deterministic, high frequency) from the rendering rate.

