### 1. The Domain Model (Strictly Typed & Frame-free)

We avoid "primitive obsession" (using raw `String` or `double` for domain concepts) by wrapping them in type-safe JVM records.

```java
package com.enterprise.orderservice.domain.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Currency;
import java.util.Objects;

// Strict Type Safety: Strongly-typed identifiers instead of raw Strings
public record OrderId(String value) {
    public OrderId {
        Objects.requireNonNull(value, "Order ID cannot be null");
        if (value.isBlank()) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Order ID cannot be empty");
    }
}

public record CustomerId(String value) {
    public CustomerId {
        Objects.requireNonNull(value, "Customer ID cannot be null");
    }
}

// Strict Type Safety: Money object ensures currency alignment
public record Money(BigDecimal amount, Currency currency) {
    public Money {
        Objects.requireNonNull(amount, "Amount cannot be null");
        Objects.requireNonNull(currency, "Currency cannot be null");
        if (amount.compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) < 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Amount cannot be negative");
        }
    }
}
```

#### Order Entity with Type-Safe State Transitions
```java
package com.enterprise.orderservice.domain.model;

import java.util.List;

public record Order(
    OrderId id,
    CustomerId customerId,
    List<OrderLineItem> items,
    Money totalAmount,
    OrderState state
) {
    public Order withState(OrderState newState) {
        return new Order(this.id, this.customerId, this.items, this.totalAmount, newState);
    }
}

public record OrderLineItem(String sku, int quantity, Money unitPrice) {}

// Strict Type Safety: Compile-time checked state machine
public enum OrderState {
    PENDING,
    ERP_SYNCED,
    FAILED_ERP_SYNC,
    COMPLETED
}
```

