### 6. Enterprise Observability & Error Handling

Large enterprises require structured error reporting (RFC 7807 standards) so monitoring tools (such as Datadog, Splunk, or Dynatrace) can automatically digest failures.

```java
package com.enterprise.orderservice.infrastructure.api;

import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.ProblemDetail;
import org.springframework.web.bind.MethodArgumentNotValidException;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.ExceptionHandler;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestControllerAdvice;

import java.net.URI;
import java.time.Instant;

@RestControllerAdvice
public class EnterpriseExceptionHandler {

    // RFC 7807 Standardized error responses
    @ExceptionHandler(MethodArgumentNotValidException.class)
    public ProblemDetail handleValidationErrors(MethodArgumentNotValidException ex) {
        ProblemDetail problemDetail = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(
                HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST, 
                "Invalid input payload sent to corporate microservice"
        );
        problemDetail.setTitle("Constraint Violation");
        problemDetail.setType(URI.create("https://api.corporate.com/errors/bad-request"));
        problemDetail.setProperty("timestamp", Instant.now());
        problemDetail.setProperty("errors", ex.getBindingResult().getFieldErrors().stream()
                .map(err -> err.getField() + ": " + err.getDefaultMessage())
                .toList());

        return problemDetail;
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(IllegalArgumentException.class)
    public ProblemDetail handleDomainTypeViolations(IllegalArgumentException ex) {
        ProblemDetail problemDetail = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(
                HttpStatus.UNPROCESSABLE_ENTITY, 
                ex.getMessage()
        );
        problemDetail.setTitle("Domain Constraints Violation");
        problemDetail.setType(URI.create("https://api.corporate.com/errors/domain-violation"));
        problemDetail.setProperty("timestamp", Instant.now());
        return problemDetail;
    }
}
```

### Why this design works for an enterprise:
1. **No Null Pointer Exceptions**: Value-object constructors (`OrderId`, `Money`) aggressively throw exceptions if invalid or null data is passed, stopping issues at the boundary.
2. **Immutable States**: By using Java `record`s, the internal state of data structures cannot be mutated unexpectedly during processing.
3. **Decoupled Architecture**: Domain models contain pure business logic and do not import any external dependencies (such as Kafka, databases, or Spring controllers). This makes them 100% testable and resilient to framework upgrades.

To demonstrate an enterprise-grade backend microservice, we will use **Java 21** with **Spring Boot 3.x**. This setup leverages modern JVM features (like strongly-typed records and pattern matching) along with enterprise standards for resilience, validation, and integration.

