### 1. Strict Type Safety: Domain Value Objects
Instead of passing raw `String` variables for IDs, we wrap them in compilation-safe Value Objects. This ensures an `EmployeeId` cannot be accidentally passed into a method expecting a `DepartmentId`.

```java
package com.enterprise.profileservice.domain;

import java.io.Serializable;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;

/**
 * Strict Value Object representing an Enterprise Employee ID.
 * Enforces corporate ID schema (e.g., EMP-12345) at construction time.
 */
public record EmployeeId(String value) implements Serializable {
    private static final Pattern VALID_PATTERN = Pattern.compile("^EMP-\\d{5}$");

    public EmployeeId {
        if (value == null || value.isBlank()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Employee ID cannot be null or blank");
        }
        if (!VALID_PATTERN.matcher(value).matches()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid Corporate ID format. Must match 'EMP-XXXXX'");
        }
    }
}
